+\appendix
+
+\section{Notebook 01: fitting one isotherm}
+\label{app:si_notebook_01}
+
+\noindent\textbf{File:} \texttt{01\_isotherm\_fitting.ipynb}.
+
+\begin{bluebox}{Purpose of this notebook}
+	Use this notebook when the starting point is one experimental isotherm:
+	a table of equilibrium concentration \(C_e\) and adsorption capacity
+	\(q_e\). Its job is to fit the candidate isotherm equations, compare
+	the fitted curves visually, and extract the universal descriptors from
+	each converged model.
+\end{bluebox}
+
+\subsection*{Input file}
+
+Place a \texttt{.csv}, \texttt{.xlsx}, or \texttt{.xls} file in the notebook
+folder. The file must contain at least two columns:
+\begin{itemize}
+	\item \texttt{Ce}: equilibrium concentration, in one declared unit such as
+	\(\mathrm{mg\,L^{-1}}\) or \(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}\);
+	\item \texttt{qe}: adsorbed amount, in one declared unit such as
+	\(\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\).
+\end{itemize}
+Column names are treated case-insensitively. The notebook removes missing
+values and keeps only \(C_e>0\) and \(q_e\geq 0\).
+
+\subsection*{How to run it}
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+	\item Set \texttt{DATA\_FILE} to the experimental file name.
+	\item Run the import and data-loading cells.
+	\item Run the fitting cell. The notebook fits all implemented models by
+	non-linear least squares; the equations are those listed in
+	Section~\ref{sec:si_models}.
+	\item Inspect the fit plot and residual plot before accepting the numerical
+	ranking.
+	\item Read the descriptor table. The reportable descriptors are
+	\(\{Q_{\max},K_{\mathrm{aff}}^{\ast},E_{\mathrm{ads}},\sigma_H\}\).
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\begin{greenbox}{What to look at first}
+	For a materials reader, the first-pass answer is: which model fits
+	without systematic residuals, what \(Q_{\max}\) is obtained, whether
+	\(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) is comparable to related systems, and whether
+	\(\sigma_H\) is close to zero or clearly heterogeneous.
+\end{greenbox}
+
+\begin{redbox}{Common mistake}
+	Do not choose the model only because it gives the highest \(R^2\). A
+	more flexible model can fit noise. Use this notebook together with
+	\texttt{02\_error\_metrics\_model\_selection.ipynb}.
+\end{redbox}
+
+\section{Notebook 02: error metrics and model selection}
+\label{app:si_notebook_02}
+
+\noindent\textbf{File:} \texttt{02\_error\_metrics\_model\_selection.ipynb}.
+
+\begin{bluebox}{Purpose of this notebook}
+	Use this notebook to decide which fitted isotherm should be reported.
+	It computes absolute and relative error metrics, information criteria,
+	Akaike weights, nested-model tests, and diagnostic plots.
+\end{bluebox}
+
+\subsection*{Input file}
+
+Use the same raw isotherm file as in Appendix~\ref{app:si_notebook_01}.
+The same \(C_e\) and \(q_e\) requirements apply. The notebook refits the
+models internally so that all metrics are computed from the same dataset and
+the same candidate set.
+
+\subsection*{How to run it}
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+	\item Set \texttt{DATA\_FILE}.
+	\item Run all fitting and metric cells.
+	\item Rank models using AIC$_c$ as the primary statistical criterion.
+	\item Check whether models within \(\Delta\)AIC$_c<2\) are physically
+	distinguishable by their descriptor values and energy distributions.
+	\item Use F-tests only for nested model pairs, such as Langmuir versus
+	Sips, and only after confirming that both models converged sensibly.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\subsection*{Output plots}
+
+The notebook produces fit overlays, residual plots, grouped metric bars,
+AIC$_c$/Akaike-weight summaries, radar charts, parity plots, heatmaps, and
+covariance-based confidence bands. These figures are diagnostic aids:
+\begin{itemize}
+	\item residual plots reveal systematic model failure;
+	\item parity plots show whether predictions follow the experimental values;
+	\item heatmaps and radar charts summarize many metrics compactly;
+	\item confidence bands show how much uncertainty is implied by the local
+	covariance matrix.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\begin{greenbox}{Decision rule}
+	Report the simplest physically meaningful model among the statistically
+	supported candidates. A lower AIC$_c$ is useful only if the resulting
+	parameters and descriptors remain physically interpretable.
+\end{greenbox}
+
+\section{Notebook 03: statistical validation}
+\label{app:si_notebook_03}
+
+\noindent\textbf{File:} \texttt{03\_statistical\_validation.ipynb}.
+
+\begin{bluebox}{Purpose of this notebook}
+	Use this notebook after a model has been selected. It asks whether the
+	fitted parameters and descriptors are stable with respect to residual
+	resampling and added measurement noise.
+\end{bluebox}
+
+\subsection*{What it tests}
+
+The notebook focuses on the Sips model as a worked example because Sips links
+directly to the homogeneous Langmuir limit through \(m\to 1\) and
+\(\sigma_H\to 0\). The same validation logic applies to other fitted models:
+\begin{itemize}
+	\item residual bootstrap gives empirical confidence intervals;
+	\item synthetic-noise tests show whether the fitted parameters drift when
+	the response is perturbed;
+	\item Monte Carlo propagation converts parameter uncertainty into
+	uncertainty in \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) and \(\sigma_H\).
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection*{How to use the result}
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+	\item Run the baseline fit.
+	\item Run the bootstrap cell and check the confidence intervals for
+	\(Q_{\max}\), \(K_S\), and \(m\).
+	\item Run the noise-sensitivity cell and check whether the parameter shift
+	is small compared with the bootstrap spread.
+	\item Report descriptor uncertainty for \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) and
+	\(\sigma_H\), not only fitted-parameter uncertainty.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\begin{redbox}{Interpretation warning}
+	A narrow confidence interval on a fitted parameter does not automatically
+	mean that the physical descriptor is robust. Always inspect propagated
+	uncertainty in \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) and \(\sigma_H\).
+\end{redbox}
+
+\section{Notebook 04: classification and clustering}
+\label{app:si_notebook_04}
+
+\noindent\textbf{File:} \texttt{04\_classification\_clustering.ipynb}.
+
+\begin{bluebox}{Purpose of this notebook}
+	Use this notebook when several materials, adsorbates, or operating
+	conditions have already been converted into descriptor rows. It does not
+	fit isotherms. It classifies or clusters descriptor tables.
+\end{bluebox}
+
+\subsection*{Input file}
+
+The input table should contain:
+\begin{itemize}
+	\item at least two numeric descriptor columns, preferably selected from
+	\(Q_{\max}\), \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\), \(\ln K_{\mathrm{aff}}^{\ast}\), and
+	\(\sigma_H\);
+	\item one target column named \texttt{group} for supervised classification;
+	\item optional categorical columns such as \texttt{material\_type} or
+	\texttt{isotherm\_type}.
+\end{itemize}
+If an older file contains a column named \texttt{sigma\_E}, the notebook
+renames it to \texttt{sigma\_H} for classification. This is only a
+backward-compatibility step; new tables should use \texttt{sigma\_H}.
+
+\subsection*{How to run it}
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+	\item Set \texttt{DATA\_FILE} to one of the descriptor tables.
+	\item Confirm that \texttt{TARGET\_COL = "group"} or change it to the
+	desired label column.
+	\item Run the preprocessing cells. Numeric descriptors are standardised;
+	categorical columns are one-hot encoded.
+	\item Run KNN for local-neighbour classification.
+	\item Run SVM for margin-based classification.
+	\item Run Ward clustering when no trusted labels are available or when the
+	goal is exploratory grouping.
+	\item Use PCA only as a visual summary of high-dimensional descriptor
+	space, not as a new physical model.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\begin{greenbox}{Classification versus clustering}
+	KNN and SVM require labels such as high/medium/low performance or
+	material family. Ward clustering does not require labels; it asks whether
+	the descriptor space contains natural groups.
+\end{greenbox}
+
+\begin{redbox}{Common mistake}
+	Do not mix raw fitted parameters with universal descriptors in the same
+	classification table. Use descriptors with consistent units and the same
+	declared \(C^\circ\). Otherwise, the classifier may separate unit
+	conventions rather than materials.
+\end{redbox}
+
 
